//? Title: Quicksort: actual cutoff ranges, comparisons and quadratic controls
//? Subtitle: Actual canonical histories, retained original fixtures and explicit controls
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Sorting arranges every key rather than selecting a single rank. The native
quicksort chooses median-of-three pivots, partitions strict smaller keys to the
left, recurses on the smaller side and finishes ranges of at most16 keys by
insertion sort. Its actual histories expose why the original seven-key example
never partitions. The original inputs and seeded scaling experiment remain, with
an explicit all-equal quadratic control.
)

\toc

> Mechanism and mathematical scope

Lomuto partition maintains a scanned prefix smaller than the pivot and a scanned
middle region at least as large. Placing the pivot between them establishes its
final sorted rank; sorting the two strict index ranges proves correctness
inductively. Equal keys remain on one side in this implementation.
$$ T(n)=T(k)+T(n-1-k)+\Theta(n). $$
\alttext(Partition work is linear plus the work of its two child index ranges.)
Balanced partitions give \(O(n\log n)\), while repeatedly selecting an end rank
gives \(O(n^2)\). Median-of-three improves many structured inputs but cannot
remove that worst case; all-equal keys still make the strict-less partition
maximally unbalanced. Recursing only on the smaller side halves the recursive
subproblem size, so the live call stack is \(O(\log n)\) even when time is
quadratic. The insertion cutoff changes actual small-input histories and
comparison counts.

> A tiny actual producing history

The table and native concept diagram read the same producing execution. Every
block below is a complete editable C++ program.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/quicksort_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    namespace a = dm::algorithms;
    namespace g = dm::graphics;
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    const std::vector<std::int64_t> original{9, 3, 7, 1, 8, 5, 2};
    auto t = a::quicksort_trace(original);
    const auto &s = t.steps[0];
    std::print("\n| actual range | actual insertion cutoff | actual range after | comparisons | partitions " "|\n| --- | --- | --- | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (const auto &e : t.steps)
        std::print("| [{},{}) | {} | `{}` | {} | {} |\n", e.lo, e.hi,
                   e.insertion, e.values, t.comparisons, t.partitions);

    g::Diagram d;
    d.node("n0", {0, 0}, std::format("Original seven keys\n{}", original));
    d.node("n1", {10, 0},
           std::format("Actual cutoff path\ninsertion = {}", s.insertion));
    d.node("n2", {20, 0}, std::format("Actual sorted result\n{}", t.values));
    d.edge("n0", "n1").edge("n1", "n2");
    d.save_svg("build/quicksort_tiny.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| actual range | actual insertion cutoff | actual range after | comparisons | partitions |
| --- | --- | --- | ---: | ---: |
| [0,7) | true | `[1, 2, 3, 5, 7, 8, 9]` | 18 | 0 |
\image(figures/quicksort_tiny.svg)
// result_end
