> API and interpretation

The old report narrated a separate no-cutoff recursive algorithm on seven keys.
That mirror is removed: the canonical producer actually uses insertion sort
there. The original seeded array stream and eight scaling sizes remain, and
frozen comparisons check the historical counted overload exactly. The
mathematical n log2 n curve is labelled as a reference, not measured work.
Actual trace entries record completed partitions and cutoff ranges in execution
order; the larger branch is iterated rather than recursively called. The sort is
unstable. Work caps are selected-event budgets, while ordinary in-place
signatures retain uncapped execution.

Reusable producers, fixtures and histories live in the canonical C++ library.
Captured records own their values and do not come from a separate report replay.
Selected counters and record/scalar policies are specified at their API
boundaries; they are not peak-byte or wall-time budgets. Synthetic structural
inputs are controlled fixtures for these mechanisms.

> References

- [Hoare, Quicksort (1962)|https://doi.org/10.1093/comjnl/5.1.10]

